WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.160
OK, up.

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:05.759
Dr. Anthony Foushee and other health officials
testified yesterday before the Senate.

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:11.049
There were a bunch of hijinx, including Senator
Rand Paul getting into an argument with Dr.

00:00:11.049 --> 00:00:17.060
Foushee, Texas so disliked, Dr. Fouche, his
testimony that last night Lieutenant Governor

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.710
in Texas, Dan Patrick, went on Fox News and
said, we're not going to listen to Dr. Foushee

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:22.710
anymore.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:27.400
But if you ask me, I'm listening to Foushee
before I'm listening to Dan Patrick from Texas

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.400
any day.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.619
So what did Dr. Foushee tell us?

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:32.180
Some of the things he said were obvious.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:37.780
Number one, Dr. Foushee said we are not really
doing that well.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:41.060
We are clearly not in control of the virus.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:47.420
And we could have 100000 cases a day in the
United States if things don't turn around.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.420
Here he is saying it is going to be very disturbing.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:55.540
I will guarantee you that, because when you
have an outbreak in one part of the country,

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:59.730
even though in other parts of the country,
they're doing well, they are vulnerable.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:04.330
I made that point very clearly last week at
a press conference.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:08.010
We can't just focus on those areas that are
having the surge.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:11.120
It puts the entire country at risk.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.860
We are now having 40 plus thousand new cases
a day.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:21.910
I would not be surprised if we go up to 100000
a day if this does not turn around.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:23.040
And so I am very concerned.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:31.900
So this has already gotten so bad in at least
16 states that they've paused or reversed

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:35.000
some aspects of the reopening plans.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:42.350
The hardest for the four hardest hit states
recently in terms of cases are Florida, Arizona,

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:43.770
Texas and California.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:50.650
Now, if you look at growth rate of cases rather
than absolute numbers, you actually see some

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:55.110
other states that are not getting a lot of
attention in terms of percentage growth.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.590
Each day they are climbing.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:01.450
And if the trend doesn't reverse in 15 or
so days, you would have a very different set

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:02.450
of states.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:05.240
That is the worst in the country in terms
of new cases.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:10.710
And again, the message is we could just be
at the beginning here.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:16.400
If you look at the total numbers, we cumulatively
have seen two point seven million coronavirus

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.980
cases in the United States.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:26.330
If the 16 states that are problematic keep
increasing their rate of new cases, just even

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:31.030
10 percent a week, which is we've seen much
faster increases than that.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:37.120
That two point seven million number in terms
of cumulative cases is going to go up dramatically

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:38.530
and very, very quickly.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.300
You can run the numbers yourself.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:46.540
If you look at fifteen, if you if we get to
15000 cases a day in Florida, 10000 a day

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:52.390
in Texas and California, you start seeing
that these numbers are going to add up insanely

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:53.390
quickly.

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:55.050
But the administration has moved on.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:57.990
I mean, they're the Trump administration is
just done.

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:01.880
They are talking about reopening.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.920
They're talking about return to greatness.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.780
They're talking about jobs and stocks and
all of this other stuff.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:12.950
We are having the work the of the four worst
the four worst days that we've had since the

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:16.780
beginning in terms of new cases happened in
the last five days.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:18.160
Understand that.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.790
And hilariously or tragically or I don't even
know what to call it at this point.

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:26.920
One of Trump's own hotels failed a Corona
virus hygiene audit.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.319
There's these rules in terms of hotels of
how they're supposed to be cleaning surfaces

00:03:31.319 --> 00:03:34.570
or supposed to be wiped down all of these
different rules.

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:40.540
Trump International in Manhattan was one of
the hotels that Inside Edition audited.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:44.920
Now, you know, Inside Edition, it's sort of
like pseudo news.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:49.050
But to the extent that they're telling the
truth about this, they had a correspondent

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:55.140
check into rooms, including at Trump's hotel
print, the program's logo, the Inside Edition

00:03:55.140 --> 00:04:00.970
logo on the bed sheets and pillowcases and
towels using a washable spray.

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:05.420
And you can only see that the spray under
ultraviolet light.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:09.360
But if the product is washed or wiped down,
then it goes away.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.690
And then the Inside Edition correspondent
would make it look like someone slept in the

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:14.690
room.

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:15.690
They would leave.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:16.690
Check out.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:19.790
Check back in and go back to the same room
the following night.

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:21.480
So new as far as the hotel.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.110
Knows this is a new customer.

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:27.450
A new guest, there should have been a full
cleaning done.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:34.090
Trump International reportedly changed the
bed sheets, did not change the pillowcase

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:38.470
at all, did not wipe down the desk and other
surfaces.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:42.900
So clearly, the Trump hotels are not the only
ones that are failing to meet the hygiene

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:44.240
requirements.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.630
And things are looking very, very ugly in
a lot of these industries.

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:50.700
But many states are just full steam ahead.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:56.070
So at this point, if we ask ourselves, like,
where is the ray of hope?

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:59.230
Where where is the possibility?

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:02.000
It's really in the medical field.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.570
And governors, we have to hope that governors
will show leadership, although many of them

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:08.570
are not.

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:12.740
We have to hope that people will take this
more seriously than they've been.

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:16.610
I mean, it's been months, but still a lot
of people aren't taking this seriously.

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:21.220
And we are putting a lot of emphasis on the
medical science community that they will develop

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:26.169
an effective treatment, something that's better
than just, you know, 15 percent improvement

00:05:26.169 --> 00:05:29.480
for only the sickest patients and a vaccine.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:33.090
And we're really waiting on that at this point
because our country has failed.

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:37.620
Now, I want to remind you next, I'm going
to talk about parts of the U.S. that are doing

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:40.810
well, but there are countries that have dominated
this.

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:45.270
I spoke to a friend who lives in Belgium and
is currently in France on a vacation with

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:46.690
her boyfriend.

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:47.690
Things are great there.

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:50.110
I mean, on public transit, everybody's wearing
masks.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:55.070
Restaurants are open with fewer tables indoors,
even outdoors.

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:57.960
Life has resumed very few cases now.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:59.070
They are doing it.

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:00.470
And I'm I'm envious of that.

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:01.480
I admit it.

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:06.790
Fortunately, here in Massachusetts, things
are going well, but we are judged by the worst

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:08.130
of our country.

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:12.910
We are not allowed to travel to many parts
of the world because as a country, we are

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:14.320
failing.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:20.330
And particularly with people able to come
in and out of states because that's the Constitution.

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:22.870
I'm not denying that.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:27.460
We have to talk next about the states that
are doing well.

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:30.070
How long can they actually hold on?

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:35.200
Because the worse things get in some parts
of the country, eventually it's going to bleed

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:37.260
into the states that are taking this very
seriously.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:37.430
So that let's discuss that next.

